#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2954b59f-d701-502f-963b-b62098a87c34" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Expressed Using Decimals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110c7289-9e87-588c-8839-082ce62fa8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "110c7289-9e87-588c-8839-082ce62fa8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256431} :answer-field/id #uuid "486b2df1-d1b4-528c-b25e-61fee88dc812" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256430}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ccfa9d31-45ab-5f64-a4e2-3d1fa5018f4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-10018" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $9+0.9+0.09+0.009+⋯$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=9$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{0.9}{9}=0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1-0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{0.9} \\\\  & =\\frac{90}{9} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd368582-0175-5f86-aa74-163e6a090acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd368582-0175-5f86-aa74-163e6a090acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256464} :answer-field/id #uuid "d70aba23-4e6c-5410-8e82-4fd86d1e5e21" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256463}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50d2f9b4-0b9f-53dd-8422-aae20f0d13ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-100459" :question/problem "If ${216}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{36})}^{6a-2}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $216={6}^{3}$ and $36={6}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({6}^{3})}^{3a+4}={(\\frac{1}{{6}^{2}})}^{6a-2}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}=\\frac{1}{{6}^{2(6a-2)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3(3a+4)}={6}^{-2(6a-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(6)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3a+4) & =-2(6a-2) \\\\ 9a+12 & =-12a+4 \\\\ 21a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-\\frac{8}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cfb0d3-2500-519b-978d-acfa938d00af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x(1-\\operatorname{cot}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31cfb0d3-2500-519b-978d-acfa938d00af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x(1-\\operatorname{cot}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256652} :answer-field/id #uuid "692f1fb9-ba55-500b-b7b4-7c538b1494c8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256651}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "667f744b-3445-5e28-b209-637d4bc0f280" :question/math-academy-id "q-104316" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{csc}x)+{e}^{x}(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x(1-\\operatorname{cot}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b8dff62-ea2b-5502-b1ee-11af84236123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b8dff62-ea2b-5502-b1ee-11af84236123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256655} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c669a5-2de0-52a1-99da-42dd787e5ab5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256654}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bd6438f-7499-53a3-942a-e79c9cbb8468" :question/math-academy-id "q-104437" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ if $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the derivative using the product rule:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+x(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x-x{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the derivative and get\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{\\pi /4} & =\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aab07c9-d4d4-520d-8b13-0b521041dab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9aab07c9-d4d4-520d-8b13-0b521041dab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256826} :answer-field/id #uuid "40273622-2870-5090-97f5-a4f10a95baac" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f61369c5-9b9f-545c-8059-dd0509d17c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-110539" :question/problem "If ${81}^{2a}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}$ then $a=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $81$ as ${3}^{4},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{81}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {({3}^{4})}^{2a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}} \\\\ {3}^{8a} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{a-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{8a} & ={3}^{-(a-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8a & =-(a-1) \\\\ 8a & =-a+1 \\\\ 9a & =1 \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538fbf14-cdc0-5657-af83-f8c31618f4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "538fbf14-cdc0-5657-af83-f8c31618f4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257157} :answer-field/id #uuid "856d08f2-0c65-5782-b962-59c19dfd35d8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cee486c2-ed27-5e12-b275-9a37215d5458" :question/math-academy-id "q-11603" :question/problem "If $y=3{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}$, find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={({x}^{3}-1)}^{4}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{'}(x)=12{x}^{2}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(6x)(({x}^{3}-1)^{4})+(3{x}^{2})(12{x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =6x({x}^{3}-1)^{4}+36{x}^{4}{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6x({x}^{3}-1)+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(6{x}^{4}-6x+36{x}^{4})\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =(42{x}^{4}-6x)\\,{({x}^{3}-1)}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $x=1$, we substitute $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=1} & =(42\\cdot {1}^{4}-6\\cdot 1){({1}^{3}-1)}^{3} \\\\  & =36\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fafff35-16a7-559e-8fbb-ab3d8bf452fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fafff35-16a7-559e-8fbb-ab3d8bf452fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257197} :answer-field/id #uuid "60164baf-f1d4-55ba-bdb3-78c9cb360971" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257196}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e51688-a8c1-51b7-9a2a-9013aa2e555b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117224" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, the value $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ is not the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ded3f952-61f0-5925-b863-e6ac4a7f0ea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257200} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e7a484-226e-585a-9c51-6c1fdb3b87b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257201} {:answer/id #uuid "6d4c73ce-b35e-58d9-a14f-bb9a593ff60c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257202} {:answer/id #uuid "44d360d7-afd6-5a05-b302-8665c4b431ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257203} {:answer/id #uuid "87ff9f03-9dce-54ed-8b24-c3705b8c5234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257204} {:answer/id #uuid "28e0e9fb-1ae9-5291-bc57-3cc1aa0f925d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257205} {:answer/id #uuid "17fbacbb-2c9d-5dfc-b8a3-7f5d9f890660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "f060392e-58ca-576a-b9ff-523b9bff84f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f060392e-58ca-576a-b9ff-523b9bff84f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257207} :answer-field/id #uuid "521d14d3-bb5b-59f1-841d-5e4bfc9a8b8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb4b0773-22d6-5ea4-882b-9ee12f384d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-117257" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$\nIII. - $(\\pi ,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\pi ,-1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-1,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\pi ,-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1587e39e-c7bf-5385-b726-a1b497f7c772" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a40c3e6-33ed-5435-94e0-b07c6d7b3520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a40c3e6-33ed-5435-94e0-b07c6d7b3520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257233} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bdbc2fc-3af5-5eaa-a32a-7a2244e463f1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257232}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af21f9ad-d3fc-504f-8809-fa4c9ea3ace4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117339" :question/problem "What values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4}{9}$ is in the range of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d44d8b18-33a5-5a24-b9bd-673cc51f97b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257236} {:answer/id #uuid "58c68d78-b88a-515f-83c6-1c3d4bbca69c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257237} {:answer/id #uuid "f5724fe7-92ec-56ed-a3bc-f56af12e586e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257238} {:answer/id #uuid "99b79ba0-fff4-5683-abf9-c0c4c9a1e11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257239} {:answer/id #uuid "4f137b7e-1902-5bdc-a1d0-dc1767719059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} {:answer/id #uuid "2071b92f-10b1-5ac3-a15c-76aba9bb7fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257241} {:answer/id #uuid "995ae627-9d8b-576a-bcee-28576e7ed6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257242} {:answer/id #uuid "63618dd2-0e11-5abc-82d7-1a61bb98c70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63618dd2-0e11-5abc-82d7-1a61bb98c70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257243} :answer-field/id #uuid "a87b1ada-f070-5a16-8efc-b76311bcca9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257235}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e42e861e-a315-5ce3-9bfd-ad15ea97bc42" :question/math-academy-id "q-117373" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$\nII. - $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(0,\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\pi $ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3235ef37-0ffb-5843-b119-69f7fbde2c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257296} {:answer/id #uuid "df5f3e74-890b-5156-ba8c-31602fb54a0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257297} {:answer/id #uuid "258eb886-5afd-548a-957e-221d02dc85f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257298} {:answer/id #uuid "4e023746-ca48-5ec4-98d8-26a7b685ac4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257299} {:answer/id #uuid "ee38a5c3-7fe8-556b-bb81-d972d9343ec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257300} {:answer/id #uuid "471dddbc-f346-5688-a28d-86d6ac2d38da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd83e73-697a-5565-aede-9bf0a4362b1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257302} {:answer/id #uuid "d79bd66a-a044-5058-9373-524b6b028706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "471dddbc-f346-5688-a28d-86d6ac2d38da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257301} :answer-field/id #uuid "d629f59a-43b8-52d9-9357-8e959cb4368d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3b6aeaa-a451-5b80-8bb6-433e2232b048" :question/math-academy-id "q-118863" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${y}^{'}-8{e}^{y}=2$\nII. - $y{y}^{'}+3=\\operatorname{cos}y$\nIII. - $x{y}^{'}-8={e}^{y}$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}-8{e}^{y} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =8{e}^{y}+2 \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=8{e}^{y}+2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{'}+3 & =\\operatorname{cos}y \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}y-3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{'}-8 & ={e}^{y} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}+8}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe07d2f2-a8b3-5d12-b95b-fba8dbab9c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-k(1+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe07d2f2-a8b3-5d12-b95b-fba8dbab9c78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-k(1+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257582} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e08717-22ce-5e3d-8f3d-8d563bc237e8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257581}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1eaf8363-468f-5d8e-a0bf-40b4da507827" :question/math-academy-id "q-123946" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-2n).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=-2n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =-2(1)=-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =-2(2)=-4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2(3)=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2$ and ${a}_{1}=-2.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=-2k$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(-2+(-2k)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{k(2+2k)}{2} \\\\  & =-k(1+k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "636ed161-bef0-57c9-bfc5-0998f85dc473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k(1-3k)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "636ed161-bef0-57c9-bfc5-0998f85dc473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k(1-3k)}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257585} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85ead7-ad6d-5eb5-ae2b-cddb4a29e87f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257584}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88a08d4f-2fec-5f88-b2ca-6305b4e3802f" :question/math-academy-id "q-123947" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-3n).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=2-3n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2-3(1)=-1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2-3(2)=-4 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2-3(3)=-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-3$ and ${a}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=2-3k$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(-1+(2-3k)) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}(1-3k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k(1-3k)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d6bb40d-91af-55ae-ab0c-fbf5b6d0f0e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Sum of an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dcbcdb3-5e2f-56b9-bcfa-9b3fa533e8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{111}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dcbcdb3-5e2f-56b9-bcfa-9b3fa533e8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{111}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257588} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47e73b7-93d8-592f-8c7f-093290c1c619" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257587}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08572949-7072-5fd4-8efb-71e1ae8b0ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-123953" :question/problem "Determine the third partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{3}{4}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{3}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{3}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{r}^{3})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{[\\frac{3}{4}]}^{3})}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{27}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{37}{64})}{(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{111}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fb046bf-0e86-518e-9e56-3a39bf560af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-K\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fb046bf-0e86-518e-9e56-3a39bf560af0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-K\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257610} :answer-field/id #uuid "1538003d-0c56-57eb-b9d4-0240798a4933" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3facbd4e-9556-5051-9eb1-216d555a418e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124072" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(1-K)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nTherefore, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(1-K)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis divergent when\n\n$$\n1-K\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d138d2c-3295-5eae-990d-42711f5792f1" :knowledge-point/title "Analyzing Statements Involving Sums of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93784fbc-83fc-552e-bca4-b879f0cba48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257623} {:answer/id #uuid "39dc14bc-8f7b-5dc0-8c25-db4ce0daa200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257624} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f6245f-990b-5cb6-ac0d-2f2ad6dedd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257625} {:answer/id #uuid "58f239de-e4fd-55ef-a60a-235d023bd6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257626} {:answer/id #uuid "513ed02a-8b72-53e8-ab93-d7cda8f774bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257627} {:answer/id #uuid "eb95d64c-4c75-5942-9e5f-6610b98a0140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257628} {:answer/id #uuid "5f45a70f-c513-545e-9bae-0dea0270c330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257629} {:answer/id #uuid "11df1ff9-b048-5c6b-afa7-e4921a4772e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f239de-e4fd-55ef-a60a-235d023bd6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257626} :answer-field/id #uuid "907ac348-0d31-53aa-a507-4f3224e0eb36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257622}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0132ee2-97f1-5701-b78e-440561d08c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-124086" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $-{10}^{500}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=25}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}-{10}^{500}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the properties of convergent series:\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }K\\cdot {a}_{n}=K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\n- If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=k}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also convergent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement II is false. Removing a finite number of terms from a convergent series gives a convergent series.\n\n- Statement III is false. The sum of two convergent series is convergent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8c91737-753a-5b7e-af1c-19314d43919a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8c91737-753a-5b7e-af1c-19314d43919a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257692} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b1fc7d-8f4a-5ba5-be9f-ebcd2a012b8d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e7cb14-a9b2-529b-981c-2ef8caf295b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124575" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=10$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1-(-0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1+0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000}{125} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd517d52-a809-52ca-bfb2-6a41f1050cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd517d52-a809-52ca-bfb2-6a41f1050cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257695} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9f4cc6b-4a73-57ba-92d8-20124206b052" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257694}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5e58701-9bd7-5e77-97f9-f02a87ad3341" :question/math-academy-id "q-124577" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a3ac2e8-a0b4-5eec-a2cb-2e94e0572103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a3ac2e8-a0b4-5eec-a2cb-2e94e0572103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257701} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e203945-6008-5260-8b6a-c5753324154c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257700}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00c8c66f-75df-508a-b3d1-06c349afffda" :question/math-academy-id "q-124582" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}{15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }15{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acbbefc-1b16-5c59-8b6e-56d7e6481078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4acbbefc-1b16-5c59-8b6e-56d7e6481078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257707} :answer-field/id #uuid "82825abf-7a8b-5dfe-821a-e491924b1552" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257706}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d9ee369-331b-5825-8ec6-ff1d89ebe2f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-124586" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}=16(\\frac{1}{16})=1 \\\\ {a}_{5} & =16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}=16(\\frac{1}{32})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }16{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42da0ddb-f88f-5ff5-8e44-3d15fee0a2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42da0ddb-f88f-5ff5-8e44-3d15fee0a2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257740} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c76489-c2c5-5932-a0c5-2b6042b00049" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257739}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1070fac-dc84-5279-ab3e-7a24f1bc3b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-124893" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=(0.75)^{4-3}=0.75\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{5}=(0.75)^{5-3}={0.75}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{{0.75}^{2}}{0.75}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }(0.75)^{n-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.75}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.75}{0.25} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series with Integer Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d133462b-1549-5105-a881-96650fbe79ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d133462b-1549-5105-a881-96650fbe79ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257765} :answer-field/id #uuid "db64e579-2ebf-5f08-88cd-da538103aeb4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257764}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4212a544-54a4-5b52-9acc-dd7e93b7e947" :question/math-academy-id "q-125247" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2{n}^{3}}\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3912c428-8308-54b9-90a5-343e36b58e8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257768} {:answer/id #uuid "a229ba77-fdb2-518d-85ef-4ca70a20e0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257769} {:answer/id #uuid "9c355002-1b19-50ea-85dc-9abb1f0d9285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257770} {:answer/id #uuid "3018a463-e4d9-528d-ba7f-3117f1126da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257771} {:answer/id #uuid "0d42ce8b-b675-535e-a014-9de87dc59b8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257772} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd98ab1-5c6a-574c-a40b-91694bdb0f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} {:answer/id #uuid "1ed076b8-4cfc-5d46-88a3-654a35b95d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257774} {:answer/id #uuid "a547bd5d-34b0-54f5-bf2e-6957451f460e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dd98ab1-5c6a-574c-a40b-91694bdb0f1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57bfeb9-ff6f-5f21-8379-8a644a1e8993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79f1c9d5-2149-5539-a2fe-8aa88505e603" :question/math-academy-id "q-125257" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{2}}} & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n\\cdot {n}^{2/3}} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=4.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt{n}} & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{1/2}} \\\\  & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/6}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7557e630-127d-5696-8ab5-f9e3f6fbb3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257778} {:answer/id #uuid "713545a3-ea7f-5282-88c9-39d02b45d63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257779} {:answer/id #uuid "e07ef159-94a5-5f67-ad2d-7ba9fa030ddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257780} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcfc2fe-609c-58e1-9d4b-c1988e64214d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257781} {:answer/id #uuid "f26d8e21-045f-5648-a5d4-d094704688fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257782} {:answer/id #uuid "0d774096-d79a-51b4-acd0-450f7b2e87cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257783} {:answer/id #uuid "e2f42e84-8872-5e2f-bf07-cd1bc26b38c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257784} {:answer/id #uuid "520a6840-1d65-591b-a8ce-50874a28abf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "520a6840-1d65-591b-a8ce-50874a28abf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257785} :answer-field/id #uuid "6458c492-81ff-5d30-91f6-062ce4df0fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257777}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "71e47f74-abbf-5c16-a637-21fb96159208" :question/math-academy-id "q-125396" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3n}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{5/4}} \\\\  & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/4}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{4{n}^{3}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}\n$$\n\n \nand this is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{n}}{\\sqrt[5]{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{1/2}}{{n}^{3/5}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/10}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series divergess by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7819d449-01e9-5b16-a26a-433627b519ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257788} {:answer/id #uuid "9d253ade-e26c-5024-98c2-c679cd173dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257789} {:answer/id #uuid "2918cd0c-06c0-5882-9896-90f1887dbc68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257790} {:answer/id #uuid "e4953611-76ff-5149-b100-68d565619ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257791} {:answer/id #uuid "13ba2729-bc00-58be-9786-4da25b30bf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257792} {:answer/id #uuid "453f20c4-494d-50fe-8398-c5c545279843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257793} {:answer/id #uuid "59f9339a-fb24-56d6-816d-d28aa94c5e16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2ee59a-25b0-56bb-adc8-e30a6848104a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453f20c4-494d-50fe-8398-c5c545279843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257793} :answer-field/id #uuid "334686ca-1c54-5ac9-b7f3-b0b671401645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257787}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8a9197c-7c16-580e-b5da-6dee677da60d" :question/math-academy-id "q-125397" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{n}^{5}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{7}}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}}.$ Rewriting this series gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=2.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{{n}^{5}}=\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5}}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=5.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\sqrt[3]{n}}{\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{7}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n\\cdot {n}^{1/3}}{{n}^{7/4}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{12}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f261c3b-6f2b-5baa-98fb-0cb62c8fc8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f261c3b-6f2b-5baa-98fb-0cb62c8fc8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257798} :answer-field/id #uuid "51328e83-e134-5598-8043-8d926c65281a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86ca18b4-98eb-5ab0-9943-316111c50313" :question/math-academy-id "q-125403" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{q-2}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=q-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\nq-2>1\\,⟹\\,q>3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "821eea6e-54b0-5192-84d9-7a02c98fde64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "821eea6e-54b0-5192-84d9-7a02c98fde64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258040} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5cdc8b7-6bee-5aa6-a18a-5b3e707ce5bb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258039}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2feeaf1-eaac-5dbf-95f2-bd5007588656" :question/math-academy-id "q-13607" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccc69ce-cc94-5927-8983-c3352e5d71af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ccc69ce-cc94-5927-8983-c3352e5d71af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258122} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bdd2145-2ded-52c3-b907-960924b52d8d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258121}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6a632e6-465f-5f69-9c0a-6eab5a5d00ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-14177" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{n}^{2}\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2+1/2}}=2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fbe7c5f-0d0f-5cb0-8356-4e360240f7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fbe7c5f-0d0f-5cb0-8356-4e360240f7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258128} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8af9183-c36f-5740-b5e0-1eae179145e4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258127}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "406bddc0-a5c1-5c9c-a58e-9ea56b088d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-14183" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[7]{{n}^{6}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{6/7}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{6}{7}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2b368c-30ff-533a-a028-f9520127f7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2b368c-30ff-533a-a028-f9520127f7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258131} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a118c03-c82c-5554-b440-65048e0ca485" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60fbd2f4-e4f7-5263-9d84-acda6fd29fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14186" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{n}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n9{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1$, the series is divergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $9$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Monotonic Polynomial and Rational Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b19ac9-5946-5486-8f0c-255642561a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258186} {:answer/id #uuid "daa7bee9-9d86-5eaf-bb3e-a098a4f9a952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258187} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8f8710-c311-53fb-8e05-09ef68f11bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258188} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a01c21-7c97-554c-b4cc-4f0b9551d88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258189} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd8e7b9-989a-565a-989d-3b186c2d6b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258190} {:answer/id #uuid "2e40f366-546c-52fe-86b2-d49a6d72fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258191} {:answer/id #uuid "2180a959-e016-537a-92ef-d275bfc73a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258192} {:answer/id #uuid "26c0d665-c3af-5a56-9f89-85e80babc162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e40f366-546c-52fe-86b2-d49a6d72fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258191} :answer-field/id #uuid "0925d6eb-4b1e-563a-b56e-57bf1b55bfe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4be75c59-4ccf-5cca-98bb-74c5dc036895" :question/math-academy-id "q-143194" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=4{n}^{6}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=4{n}^{6}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(n+1)^{6}}{4{n}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{6}}{{n}^{6}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that \n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Monotonic Polynomial and Rational Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9faf36f-d5c0-5429-8740-32d1a5c32e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258196} {:answer/id #uuid "3d21c5dd-cc0b-5b2c-b6e1-64843eed4823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258197} {:answer/id #uuid "e2902026-1882-5969-b07f-1402cf2309a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258198} {:answer/id #uuid "36fc3e07-67bd-5f21-90c6-f99647d25d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258199} {:answer/id #uuid "29cb7f46-285c-5637-b3c8-4ddf6730bc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258200} {:answer/id #uuid "b6435d47-f7bf-5948-a941-0de3a9f5cafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258201} {:answer/id #uuid "7433c384-9613-5f54-a21a-f194e850efe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258202} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaad856-45db-5d10-8730-1a550595f04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccaad856-45db-5d10-8730-1a550595f04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebfaf26-9321-5379-bb3f-c467ba5f5f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3210b6e-fd27-5b2e-a30b-59e4d6036658" :question/math-academy-id "q-143195" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=7{n}^{4}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=7{n}^{4}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(n+1)^{4}}{7{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{4}}{{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8afe1fbe-a370-5f9c-8204-5194bbe383c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8afe1fbe-a370-5f9c-8204-5194bbe383c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258409} :answer-field/id #uuid "4405a727-6ade-5d8e-a85a-bfa6dc0258d6" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258408} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70316078-ad4e-5c9b-92b5-da3f6da52993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70316078-ad4e-5c9b-92b5-da3f6da52993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258411} :answer-field/id #uuid "04f8bf00-2bcd-575d-be1f-42ca13a8d44e" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258410}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa079b5f-e6f6-5243-ab92-5790c7d64511" :question/math-academy-id "q-156159" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, what is the condition for the real constant $K$ guaranteeing that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is **convergent?**\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, multiplying the terms of a divergent series by $0$ gives a series that converges to $0.$\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-{K}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {K}^{2} & =2 \\\\ K & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e30e1205-c74c-594c-a807-fe9653a44b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e30e1205-c74c-594c-a807-fe9653a44b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258442} :answer-field/id #uuid "7108e05b-40b5-5609-a877-97c722210967" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258441}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a172fe0-8627-5d13-a398-073dac6cbf1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-156876" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which $\\sqrt{K}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nHowever, the expression $\\sqrt{K}$ is not defined when $K<0.$ \n\nTherefore, the series  \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{K}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent for $K\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b13adcc-2808-5309-b26f-a5be79d1e0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b13adcc-2808-5309-b26f-a5be79d1e0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258445} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d0e5ddd-5c7f-5387-a7f8-e996a7859b62" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258444}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b57d0b6-bc38-5056-bac4-445ec8a4f7ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-156904" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({K}^{3}+1)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is divergent.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({K}^{3}+1)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis divergent when\n\n$$\n{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102ce3e3-dc0c-5390-abd0-d647367d0d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "102ce3e3-dc0c-5390-abd0-d647367d0d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258619} :answer-field/id #uuid "842e9247-ae0a-5719-b90b-9aa641981a7f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258618}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cea81d37-e8da-5ed2-8186-38548c31655d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16699" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(1)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{3}(5{x}^{2}+2).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{2}+2))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2))\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =-30x{cos}^{2}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{'}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(1) & =-30(1){cos}^{2}(5(1)^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5(1)^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aabe493-35ea-5f67-8298-28db0b078074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aabe493-35ea-5f67-8298-28db0b078074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258770} :answer-field/id #uuid "33139f29-20c3-5e6c-a620-3ab2dd74f087" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49490cb9-3ce1-5534-a5d6-1fffc1caa530" :question/math-academy-id "q-1883" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-2}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{-3}+(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-6{x}^{-3}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d5ee2a-8c82-5e86-92da-fcd172396fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d5ee2a-8c82-5e86-92da-fcd172396fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258791} :answer-field/id #uuid "54c917c5-7d48-5935-8b3a-9bf7e3ba9958" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "075f5bd8-8f8e-5a44-8865-d55c0e9f72f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-19262" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25e78d632f4bbe23ebbf700946b1a05a6b4f9f578c1d8047ddb067842a820fa9.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}x,$ and the point ${A}^{'}$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 6,\\operatorname{ln}6)=(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bfad614-2b1c-5d61-a877-1547f550341a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bfad614-2b1c-5d61-a877-1547f550341a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258797} :answer-field/id #uuid "42ed291c-f172-57c1-945a-5a2c11b77b6b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258796}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "124929ec-0b47-501a-8761-14e3174bc1cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1938" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x)(4x)-(\\operatorname{sec}x)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3bf612-b227-5d72-86a1-3649210b669d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c3bf612-b227-5d72-86a1-3649210b669d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258984} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fc78f6-a9b8-5850-a792-66a2d17ff4f0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258983}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7da6583b-f41d-5231-9e7b-686d369d4d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-2400" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259408} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a47021-fb80-56ac-9acd-3b46f185f95e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4007a637-1203-5eab-9c3e-d86e260889f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-310182" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe55bba9-dd10-5bc7-9f24-e71a53b0559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe55bba9-dd10-5bc7-9f24-e71a53b0559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259432} :answer-field/id #uuid "34136657-7590-59a8-8251-ce13de8a18c7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81fa46b9-6e38-5bbb-a82c-d73296a4d0fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-31288" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a74bc4-19e8-59d6-b3c4-1f7913db2ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a74bc4-19e8-59d6-b3c4-1f7913db2ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259435} :answer-field/id #uuid "36db61fa-c70d-5a2d-8faa-8f54dc214ad6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6422b1f8-1755-59dd-a7bc-a54ffd2688c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-312907" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{8}(5{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} ${cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=8{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =8{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8{(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{3}))}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}))\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-120{x}^{2}{cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16651160-26ff-5241-9989-b9ee17f48379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\csc(10x)\\cot(10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16651160-26ff-5241-9989-b9ee17f48379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\csc(10x)\\cot(10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259441} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9926bd-6a5b-5790-a025-a5a677b25113" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259440}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01c41333-1844-5900-ad46-7ce70f99f944" :question/math-academy-id "q-31333" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ for $y =8\\csc(10x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = 8\\csc{u}, \\qquad u = 10x\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -8\\csc u \\cot u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 10.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-8\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot (10)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc u \\cot u \\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc (10x) \\cot (10x).\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5b8f9fb-c244-5126-9181-8847430c5aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b8f9fb-c244-5126-9181-8847430c5aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259444} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd475635-649d-56fe-b8a0-c5c13f9d210c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259443}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a22f8807-f06b-5bb5-ba16-a2214209db89" :question/math-academy-id "q-313362" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(3x+5)^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(3x+5)}^{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}){(3x+5)}^{2}-({x}^{3})(6(3x+5))}{{({(3x+5)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}{(3x+5)}^{2}-6{x}^{3}(3x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(3x+5)((3x+5)-2x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "937297d6-a133-550c-8cc2-602f4a7f8920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "937297d6-a133-550c-8cc2-602f4a7f8920" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259447} :answer-field/id #uuid "048e41c7-4abd-5f21-a9c0-f0b4e4a99ac2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259446}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83184978-b220-5676-82cf-9ec259139917" :question/math-academy-id "q-31341" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cot\\left(1-\\dfrac x 5 \\right)$ at the point where $x =1.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cot{u}, \\qquad u = 1-\\dfrac x 5.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc^2 u \\qquad \\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\csc^2 u \\cdot  \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2 \\left(1-\\dfrac{x}{5}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n    \\eqalign{\n\nf'(x) & =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(1-\\dfrac 1 5 \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t& =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac 4 5 \\right) .\n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is $\\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c29bc9-70da-577a-ad45-1709eb9e94ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c29bc9-70da-577a-ad45-1709eb9e94ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259450} :answer-field/id #uuid "35589321-9f7c-5ff0-87ab-ad7726d91a5b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259449}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e90a75e5-83c6-52f3-9c5a-0f176678967d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31355" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{csc}^{2}x)\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{cot}x\\cdot (2x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c318c7-19c4-55b9-ad35-06d42b8bf435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c318c7-19c4-55b9-ad35-06d42b8bf435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259456} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad46b0f-dbd5-54af-9929-d948ec59e431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ba46c76-d873-5609-acab-7d3bfa67173b" :question/math-academy-id "q-313722" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{'}(x)$ if $h(x)=4x\\operatorname{csc}(x).$\n\n${h}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{csc}(x))+(4x)(-\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88946dd7-a497-59d4-9d3a-53ea96edf878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88946dd7-a497-59d4-9d3a-53ea96edf878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259765} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7b5992c-e506-553c-a960-95b6a05f6a33" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259764}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11121e1d-3771-5b73-8d84-a5287eac4550" :question/math-academy-id "q-3362" :question/problem "If ${27}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{81})}^{-2n+8},$ then $n=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $27={3}^{3}$ and $81={3}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{3})}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}})}^{-2n+8}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the power of quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4(-2n+8)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}={3}^{-4(-2n+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2n+2) & =-4(-2n+8) \\\\ 6n+6 & =8n-32 \\\\ 2n & =38 \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4dd606-3245-5362-863c-9cd43490bd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4dd606-3245-5362-863c-9cd43490bd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259783} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0acb996-b153-55cf-bbe7-8329594e8e99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b58612b7-225f-5023-841c-cc50f36c7d9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337182" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{2}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{3}}($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}(x)\\operatorname{tan}(x))(5{x}^{2})-(3\\operatorname{sec}(x))(10x)}{(5{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}(x)\\operatorname{tan}(x)-30x\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{25{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x\\operatorname{sec}(x)(3x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-6)}{25{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(x)(3x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-6)}{5{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{3}}(x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73911311-b113-5748-be58-21901c884257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73911311-b113-5748-be58-21901c884257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259904} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc7f983-afa9-534d-9d90-007ddaf612bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c556fe2-0de3-5c83-a8df-8864a240ec1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340642" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y=g(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ if $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the product rule and sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)(\\operatorname{cot}x)+(\\operatorname{csc}x)(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}x({cot}^{2}x+{csc}^{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and evaluate the slope $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={g}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})[{cot}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot ({0}^{2}+{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a13c5fa3-74bc-547e-aafc-cf53b0e2a85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a13c5fa3-74bc-547e-aafc-cf53b0e2a85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259977} :answer-field/id #uuid "13abd639-5f6a-515e-9363-f08635a47382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28984f6e-c237-5f01-8863-8027f6429ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342405" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $f(x) = \\sec\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by $f'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. We express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\sec{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec{u}\\tan{u}, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\pi x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\sec{u}\\tan{u} \\cdot 2\\pi x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 2\\pi x \\sec{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}\\tan{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we find \n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\nf'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) &= 2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sec{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]}\\tan{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\sec{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi\\sqrt{2}.}\n\\end{eqnarray}\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb0d5e7-3cfa-5971-a047-f58f04ee2b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddb0d5e7-3cfa-5971-a047-f58f04ee2b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259980} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd9790b-7e42-5e1b-98ac-44ebce2a0ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f88db24-9e14-5959-aa53-c08754c1eb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f88db24-9e14-5959-aa53-c08754c1eb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259982} :answer-field/id #uuid "6416b67c-840b-5049-a1c1-7e1b0c065c89" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259981}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c48ab644-069c-576e-8de1-e3fed8004930" :question/math-academy-id "q-342412" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}$ at the point $(1,2).$\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}$x \\: + \\:$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot{u}  \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(-\\csc{u}\\cot{u}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(1)& =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\[5pt] \n\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\pi\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,2\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny-2 &= -\\pi\\sqrt{3}(x-1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny &= \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-\\pi\\sqrt{3}}x + \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d80b1-259c-5bc4-ba87-8ab1f5ca7b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7d80b1-259c-5bc4-ba87-8ab1f5ca7b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259985} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1c0972-ec5c-5c46-800f-b7700c4cafca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c59f39-d500-5872-99f5-af5db1f70149" :question/math-academy-id "q-342413" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = -2\\sin^{3}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -6u^2 \\cdot \\cos{(x)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a849f73b-fdf5-55c0-a59f-c2ca004c1be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a849f73b-fdf5-55c0-a59f-c2ca004c1be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259988} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0a176b-9600-5544-8caa-1de4b7d6c542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c25b5ade-5d47-5a5f-b5a6-b2a486a4775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342427" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\cos^{7}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\cos{(x)})^{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = u^{7}, \\qquad u = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm d u} = 7u^6, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 7u^6 \\cdot (-\\sin{(x)})\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-7\\cos^6{(x)}\\sin{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056fe1ad-11a3-5c3e-8d31-5680255cf264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056fe1ad-11a3-5c3e-8d31-5680255cf264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260006} :answer-field/id #uuid "32b5ed67-b70b-53e3-b2ab-9bb336284e9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b225130-6791-5c02-b157-558955b95349" :question/math-academy-id "q-343333" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{5x}\\operatorname{ln}x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{5x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{5x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=5{e}^{5x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(5{e}^{5x})(\\operatorname{ln}x)+({e}^{5x})\\,(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da596db-98ab-5d0a-9727-83ec6f492af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da596db-98ab-5d0a-9727-83ec6f492af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260073} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c737f4-f34c-5872-9cb2-c62d52005703" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260072}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6feb18f4-8454-57e9-a1f1-b1f2a3e71478" :question/math-academy-id "q-347372" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{10}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bde024-7ec9-51c5-97d1-87f23fbc0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bde024-7ec9-51c5-97d1-87f23fbc0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260079} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d16ffa-4c18-506b-81a9-7b7cd5ff8c61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b81faf4-0bdd-52be-b70d-34db9528cb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-347774" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fourth term equal to $16$ and a seventh term equal to $\\frac{16}{125}.$ Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the terms of this sequence. Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{4}=16$ and ${a}_{7}=\\frac{16}{125}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{125})}{16} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{16}{125})(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=4$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{3} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{125}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =2000(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =2500.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1478c6-3c7e-5da2-b053-0af134795669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(3-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1478c6-3c7e-5da2-b053-0af134795669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(3-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260106} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ad890a-0dcc-5b5c-a985-2f7327b87568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04a861e6-bb06-5ca0-a8f9-ed37e1946328" :question/math-academy-id "q-349085" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum ${s}_{k}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4-2n).$ Express the answer in terms of the parameter $k.$\n\n${s}_{k}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4-2n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2$ and ${a}_{1}=2.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=4-2k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(2+4-2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}(6-2k) \\\\  & =k(3-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9cd8e5-27e2-58d9-8af9-f42c3800d9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9cd8e5-27e2-58d9-8af9-f42c3800d9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260132} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e54e373-cf38-5ad3-9996-f860bc1db291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f8f961d-0c12-55d8-a6b7-610d52702173" :question/math-academy-id "q-350588" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(0.2)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3{(0.2)}^{1}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3{(0.2)}^{2}}{3{(0.2)}^{1}}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(0.2)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{1-0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a09274-3b82-53bb-92cc-b7199d2b2359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a09274-3b82-53bb-92cc-b7199d2b2359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b831cea-f9fe-5289-ab8e-950cf1dfb3ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260134}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecd963bf-5bba-5df2-996d-756760d0d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350592" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{1}=0.42.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{2}}{\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{1}}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.42}{1-0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.42}{0.7} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{70} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f810e291-0c36-56a6-a2a9-94abdfc6f33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f810e291-0c36-56a6-a2a9-94abdfc6f33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260138} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d1584c-c343-5091-b5fb-8a3f42a0ea37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a570aa2-6317-52cf-88ac-f33d0dd4de74" :question/math-academy-id "q-350593" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(0.75)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6{(0.75)}^{1}=4.5.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6{(0.75)}^{2}}{6{(0.75)}^{1}}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(0.75)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{450}{25} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a15f53-0b90-53fe-809b-00e1e651cdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a15f53-0b90-53fe-809b-00e1e651cdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260147} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6c0036-bfd5-5b86-9819-1c43e8908c53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a515033-9d33-5db6-a4a5-02cf6fa471fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350633" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2+1}=\\frac{27}{5}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3+1}=\\frac{81}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{81}{25})}{(\\frac{27}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7d861e0-b37e-5859-8382-862845635d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7d861e0-b37e-5859-8382-862845635d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260162} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2db9c67-a917-5a3d-8f0f-9bfae2afb4b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260161}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa440b09-fed9-5ae8-94fe-6509db1fed32" :question/math-academy-id "q-352290" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n}$\ngiven that \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=2$ and \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a075da18-342a-549a-b681-746e39c6bd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a075da18-342a-549a-b681-746e39c6bd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260556} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7c8e57-06af-5956-8bd8-c3cc75ad39ab" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260555}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9b3f537-d998-5b44-ac6e-d3ede45445e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48508" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = \\cos (5x-2 )$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac 2 5$ is given by $f'\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = 5x-2.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot 5\\\\[5pt]\n&=-5\\sin u\\\\[5pt]\n&= -5\\sin\\left(5x-2 \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac 25,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\n\nm=f'\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)  & =- 5 \\sin \\left(5\\cdot \\dfrac 2 5-2\\right)\\\\[5pt] \n\t\t& =- 5 \\sin 0\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 0.\n \n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\left(x-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\\\[5pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0edba3-47c2-5dee-b6e3-7adad1957fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d0edba3-47c2-5dee-b6e3-7adad1957fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260559} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b0012d-1ca0-501f-b7be-a230702e57a1" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260558}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d322c8f-a329-5374-8649-0dbb3acf87eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48510" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sec(3x)-2x$ at the point $\\left(0,1\\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\sec(3x )$ and $h(x)=-2x.$ Then,\n\n\\begin{align}\nf(x)&=g(x)+h(x) . \n\\end{align}\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\sec(3x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\ng = \\sec u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d g}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec u \\tan u \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}g}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}g}{\\textrm{d}u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\sec u \\tan u \\cdot (3)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=3 \\sec u \\tan u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                                        & =3\\sec (3x) \\tan (3x)\n}\n\n\\]\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d x} = -2\n\\]\n\nHence,\n\\[f'(x)=g'(x)+h'(x)=3\\sec (3x) \\tan (3x) -2.\\]\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align*}\nm=f'(0) & =3\\sec\\left(3\\cdot 0\\right)\\tan\\left(3\\cdot 0\\right) -2\\\\[3pt]\n\t& =3\\sec 0\\tan 0 -2\\\\[3pt]\n\t& =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 - 2\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-2.       \n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1&=-2 \\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1&=-2x\\\\[3pt]\ny&=-2x+1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5290779a-217c-5e77-a6ef-4d8a2c3e2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5290779a-217c-5e77-a6ef-4d8a2c3e2fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260565} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf82e01c-1269-551e-8e93-cecda9b6e244" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260564}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57478b30-a73d-57e4-8308-8dc05fea241e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48636" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\cos{\\left(x^3\\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,1\\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = x^3.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =3x^2.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= -\\sin{u} \\cdot 3x^2\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-3x^2\\sin(x^3).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(0) & = -3(0)^2\\sin(0^3) = 0.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\\begin{align}\ny - y_1 & = m(x - x_1)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0 \\cdot \\left(x - 0 \\right)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n\ny & =1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffd05dd0-edc0-5d5e-9bf3-895de08612ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffd05dd0-edc0-5d5e-9bf3-895de08612ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260568} :answer-field/id #uuid "f31279c8-8912-5d7a-a4fa-2d896f5d9f64" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260567}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3eae5d22-d1dc-5383-9678-4b5348d7df9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y =\\cos \\left(\\sin x \\right)$ at the point $\\left(0,1 \\right).$\n\n$y =$: {{y}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =\\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot (\\cos x)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\cos x \\sin (\\sin x).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\tf'(0)&=-\\cos (0) \\sin (\\sin (0))\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-1 \\cdot 0 \\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.\n}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1 &= (0)\\cdot\\left(x-0\\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8d98476-9d3b-54f6-aeba-86dff4ce26de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8d98476-9d3b-54f6-aeba-86dff4ce26de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260636} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bbb7d57-388c-5c2a-8023-bee8e0b4ee60" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260635}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79b3da5f-c66e-5a1e-bee9-a95c92685b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49824" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, what is the condition for the real constant $K$ guaranteeing that $K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nSo, $K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent for all $K.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae3472fc-382b-5dcd-9af1-257afd195ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae3472fc-382b-5dcd-9af1-257afd195ad9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260662} :answer-field/id #uuid "72fea3e7-a34b-57dd-a2f0-690568ee01fc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260661}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485f013d-0bc9-5843-bf53-dbb0e9f37daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-50450" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3q-1}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=3q-1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n3q-1>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b28c8366-7d9f-5ebc-b954-1186b42ed53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b28c8366-7d9f-5ebc-b954-1186b42ed53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260665} :answer-field/id #uuid "538d9ef9-6498-5e05-8579-48f1dbafb867" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260664}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "256094b3-ea33-541d-8e90-d5daa58588f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-50621" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2q-2}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=2q-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n2q-2>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a8ded69-fffa-5883-b1be-99365048eb11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a8ded69-fffa-5883-b1be-99365048eb11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260668} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87bcaed-30d0-56bb-adf0-a10cb6ec7d1d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35266cb0-18e1-5e7d-94b2-eb7099779b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-50623" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4q-1}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=4q-1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n4q-1>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f58e5-0f98-58b1-b2f7-9f0ee33ed075" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01656440-71b6-5f0b-9ae2-d49cb7994735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01656440-71b6-5f0b-9ae2-d49cb7994735" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260886} :answer-field/id #uuid "81cf18b5-a60f-5bcc-bf33-564c486ad988" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b79383d-a0fa-58be-9081-eb2564bea567" :question/math-academy-id "q-65938" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f58e5-0f98-58b1-b2f7-9f0ee33ed075" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a1bacdb-2b02-5c2b-a494-eb6aa4f0049a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a1bacdb-2b02-5c2b-a494-eb6aa4f0049a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260889} :answer-field/id #uuid "a864d3e1-1652-5a25-9295-3c0dc4974de9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9123ac6-7632-56b3-b65f-ed8e53a479f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-65940" :question/problem "What values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=-2$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260890 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cde5e5d4-8989-5920-89de-6d3900d38455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cde5e5d4-8989-5920-89de-6d3900d38455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260892} :answer-field/id #uuid "39739507-7ba7-58ad-8112-9e8b9e1d9a62" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b5a8d34-c652-5bb0-9d30-a93288635736" :question/math-academy-id "q-65944" :question/problem "What gives the $y$-intercept of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the intersection with the $y$-axis occurs at $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6b30e7-fba6-597c-8306-27d707bfc244" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting One Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089bec96-9182-5888-ac4f-ff6b790f83ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "089bec96-9182-5888-ac4f-ff6b790f83ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260986} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e9ccd4-c33e-56dc-9e3d-865956871ef6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260985}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0166c2e-fa1a-5455-a7a2-c570d25330ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-66864" :question/problem "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{x-5}={3}^{x}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${3}^{2},$ and then rewrite the left-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {({3}^{2})}^{x-5} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{2(x-5)} & ={3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-5) & =x \\\\ 2x-10 & =x \\\\ x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "847298b9-a2bc-5e6e-95ca-5505365402af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "847298b9-a2bc-5e6e-95ca-5505365402af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261033} :answer-field/id #uuid "d11d1acd-f605-5019-b2ec-43e4dae29f74" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261032}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7f6ddd3-7a07-53be-a65c-aca333beb0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-67217" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $0,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{0+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1+1}={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}}=\\frac{{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{4}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261037 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccff46d8-51f2-52c3-9d44-6f826b2af348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccff46d8-51f2-52c3-9d44-6f826b2af348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261039} :answer-field/id #uuid "12a24b21-1e39-5e3a-b35e-388b6c84b984" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261038}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c4f183b-d69c-5863-8872-aaadbc068516" :question/math-academy-id "q-67277" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $36$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{4}{3}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=36$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{3})}{36} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec14a23-1c72-554b-953c-a75bf3129a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ec14a23-1c72-554b-953c-a75bf3129a46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261045} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86636ef-8a10-5440-9ad0-7bc6b121987b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2b352d28-1238-5c21-917e-ac85575e41a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-67279" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $35$ and a fourth term equal to $-\\frac{56}{25}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=35$ and ${a}_{4}=-\\frac{56}{25}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{56}{25})}{35} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =-\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1-(-\\frac{2}{5}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1+\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(\\frac{7}{5})} \\\\  & =35\\cdot \\frac{5}{7} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "399ca58c-1a93-5aa8-a3a1-30c792e828f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399ca58c-1a93-5aa8-a3a1-30c792e828f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261051} :answer-field/id #uuid "892a22f9-897a-55b5-b400-54bbd725f0eb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261050}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8da21356-cf78-57bd-8a24-bca56a7d11af" :question/math-academy-id "q-67304" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{8}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{125})}{(\\frac{4}{25})}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{25})}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{25})}{(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f58e5-0f98-58b1-b2f7-9f0ee33ed075" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef7b4e10-2cba-5e29-91f9-ea539ed6fa33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef7b4e10-2cba-5e29-91f9-ea539ed6fa33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261237} :answer-field/id #uuid "3794aded-25f8-5635-b922-72b54ed6d48e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261236}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2c9898d-1220-5cf6-af81-cc3018702654" :question/math-academy-id "q-70744" :question/problem "What values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{5}{7}$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c393bf6-5c08-53f5-8247-bc5d657d724e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261240} {:answer/id #uuid "f97fe431-d63a-5296-b665-7da88a48dc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261241} {:answer/id #uuid "6349aaee-6305-50a4-bf23-008a5f3a5f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261242} {:answer/id #uuid "aace6f9d-4998-5209-964b-da997a689e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261243} {:answer/id #uuid "0e62c000-c8ff-52d1-bb7b-5d30a29b8198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261244} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9a4e02-1364-55d9-bb77-ff054bc06049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261245} {:answer/id #uuid "3c7e8319-f3fc-5d41-a2ab-88b41047141f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261246} {:answer/id #uuid "3256a62d-aeb1-5525-a581-299382c8cbdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e62c000-c8ff-52d1-bb7b-5d30a29b8198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261244} :answer-field/id #uuid "45350a9c-7538-5f62-8547-71134014764d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261239}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5869734-9305-599d-bf1a-57e700c7e1e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70766" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$\nII. - $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{2\\pi }{3},-\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3df2432-d0b3-55da-bb5f-06253adf8bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261250} {:answer/id #uuid "7712557a-7be5-5f7f-9c67-4d99cd9aa572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261251} {:answer/id #uuid "eedd3e10-4981-51c4-9dd9-db253d883611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261252} {:answer/id #uuid "627a980f-c83e-5930-8f39-3e3aa302d02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261253} {:answer/id #uuid "f930a2ff-2e31-57a7-94aa-f1776ffcdd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261254} {:answer/id #uuid "c29eba9f-8b94-583e-aa05-3b9512acc2ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261255} {:answer/id #uuid "0fea163c-b666-5d70-960e-245dd1e65817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261256} {:answer/id #uuid "619e2a91-10dd-555f-9125-519e91fc7680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "627a980f-c83e-5930-8f39-3e3aa302d02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261253} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3cff461-a7c4-5449-83d4-576218bc4146" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261249}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b051727c-2d33-5ba7-828f-c75227488b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-70790" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x?$\n\nI. - $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\nII. - $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\nIII. - $(\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\sqrt{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a43d7e-2dd2-5da6-acab-0f0ff1c3bbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\csc(\\cos x)\\cot(\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18a43d7e-2dd2-5da6-acab-0f0ff1c3bbfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin x\\csc(\\cos x)\\cot(\\cos x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261379} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e9ac7c-62e8-59c9-ae79-fbe9ddb29443" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4550e1e-4735-5154-97f7-9b71d5ccbc18" :question/math-academy-id "q-72170" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =\\csc ( \\cos x).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\cos{x}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{x}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot  (-\\sin x)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\sin x\\csc \\left(\\cos x\\right) \\cot \\left(\\cos x\\right).\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f70d6425-40b5-5368-89f9-83f34c02138d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f70d6425-40b5-5368-89f9-83f34c02138d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261463} :answer-field/id #uuid "576cae7c-5cc8-55f3-8375-8c9b26fe0c07" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261462}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2395de7-c9eb-54cc-9c43-efe503db4a0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7311" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/95d77b35c2647bc98480dbe24692c3af3c0fe004a4e91b272d10ad0a4c5ebaae.png)\n\nGiven the diagram above, what is the value of $k?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=k{log}_{4}x$ can be obtained from $y={log}_{4}x$ by a vertical stretch of scale factor $k.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(4,1)$ that lies on $y={log}_{4}x$ becomes\n\n$$\n(4,k\\cdot 1)=(4,4)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=k{log}_{4}x.$ \n\nTherefore, $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8a9c045-18f6-58c4-9746-9a119ed6975c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8609813b-c478-5c76-bd43-047e84569dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8609813b-c478-5c76-bd43-047e84569dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261554} :answer-field/id #uuid "565b2039-da22-5560-b99a-7fec4f3a711b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261553}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "78423b17-d99e-5bed-be25-87a38ce33c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-74697" :question/problem "Find the eighth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n-4).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=2n-4.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2(1)-4=-2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =2(2)-4=0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =2(3)-4=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=2$ and first term ${a}_{1}=-2.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{8}=2(8)-4=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{8}=\\frac{8}{2}(-2+12)=40.\n$$\n\nSo the eighth partial sum of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2n-4)$ is equal to $40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8a9c045-18f6-58c4-9746-9a119ed6975c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10586054-8fdf-5832-b00a-5540918bd9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10586054-8fdf-5832-b00a-5540918bd9c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d388395a-c18c-55ac-b22c-054f838d67e9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261556}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73bb57e1-72df-5ec1-b6e1-ed439e307054" :question/math-academy-id "q-74698" :question/problem "Find the sixth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6n-1).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=6n-1.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =6(1)-1=5 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =6(2)-1=11 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =6(3)-1=17,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we notice that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=6$ and first term ${a}_{1}=5.$ \n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{6}=6(6)-1=35,$ we get\n\n$$\n{s}_{6}=\\frac{6}{2}(5+35)=120.\n$$\n\nSo the sixth partial sum of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(6n-1)$ is equal to $120.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d6bb40d-91af-55ae-ab0c-fbf5b6d0f0e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Sum of an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5773536-b326-539e-b846-ebfaae094297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5773536-b326-539e-b846-ebfaae094297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261560} :answer-field/id #uuid "38a6cbff-71f0-55f6-9efd-835e6ee70e7c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261559}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685b71b2-628c-518e-91ff-af651d3035a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-74710" :question/problem "Determine the fourth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{4}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{r}^{4})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{81})}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}\\cdot \\frac{80}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{80}{81}) \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ead28b84-1f92-510d-87b3-07eb9a394622" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series with Integer Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e2b1f65-a46d-549e-873d-5a228f09e45c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261781} {:answer/id #uuid "603d1924-96a6-5d2b-ae86-df2ef4c4144e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "603d1924-96a6-5d2b-ae86-df2ef4c4144e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Converges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261782} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0b0645-835e-53dd-911e-0a6a3bfbcc85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261780}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc370e1e-b2df-5215-9a8e-5cac451a46d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81422" :question/problem "Use the $p$-test to determine whether the series below converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{7}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given series is a $p$-series with $p=7.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23d0168c-6ede-54fb-9652-f394884c4e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261791} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0b5d61-6fa5-5dbe-bbbd-c9da34c952d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261792} {:answer/id #uuid "99718659-6b95-56c5-93fa-2b928cdebb4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261793} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4adb4b-d7a3-56f6-8d9c-96e044bb71c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261794} {:answer/id #uuid "32969826-446c-54d6-b26d-0e9590c471a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261795} {:answer/id #uuid "aff89f37-bb34-5b98-97a9-6b06bf8214f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261796} {:answer/id #uuid "f643ff22-3868-5aa5-8920-ba5f8b8db4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261797} {:answer/id #uuid "94930072-171e-5522-8c48-ceede9eb52fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aff89f37-bb34-5b98-97a9-6b06bf8214f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261796} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c8bf6f-5303-5a12-a675-f73664df7044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f47715f-32fb-5b55-bda6-7c06af34e3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81430" :question/problem "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- First, we look at ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}.$ Rewriting this gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the second series, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{n}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/4}},\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series converges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{\\sqrt{{n}^{3}}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{{n}^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{1/2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6051f35-c390-57ba-8448-40f53f11c1c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Which Series Converge From a List"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c9c4a7e-172c-5151-b005-2f18d4b7f553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261801} {:answer/id #uuid "a4905309-acf2-54f0-8ad1-8c3693d19495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261802} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffb9354-834b-5e1e-ae5b-04ec4bf67261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261803} {:answer/id #uuid "30283cbb-7e38-5130-a664-a545053caeaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261804} {:answer/id #uuid "3425748c-4498-5dc4-8bdf-67d081fee3c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261805} {:answer/id #uuid "d49e6dcf-48a0-5db3-83a8-312ebf38293b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261806} {:answer/id #uuid "99d4cd90-a9ad-5b81-a7cc-b8b3a67eacac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261807} {:answer/id #uuid "45b7d340-5474-55a3-8cc9-b87c7cc44aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4905309-acf2-54f0-8ad1-8c3693d19495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261802} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f4075f-7219-516b-b866-7b0a6ea4dbed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a79cd092-c0f5-50bf-ba4a-1bcea68a7322" :question/math-academy-id "q-81488" :question/problem "Which of the following series are convergent?\n\nI. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$\nII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}$\nIII. - ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{7}}}$" :question/worked-solution "We analyze each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}$ is a $p$-series with $p=1$ (the harmonic series). Since $p\\le 1,$ the series diverges by the $p$-series test.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}$ is a $p$-series with $p=3.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\n- For the third series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{7}}} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{7/3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{7}{3}.$ Since $p>1,$ the series is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd5c7225-34e0-5ea9-b054-d5528d842d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c7225-34e0-5ea9-b054-d5528d842d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261811} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1353fc3-e021-52a3-800e-a88a9f705dfd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261810}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79d71a30-04c5-5ca5-8156-b0027fdb06ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-81489" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{q+1}}$ converge?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=q+1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\nq+1>1\\,⟹\\,q>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333eb865-1aff-5b94-a5c8-384293d669d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "333eb865-1aff-5b94-a5c8-384293d669d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262000} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b342020-669f-5e04-8b85-dfad9597da56" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261999}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9c004d-c216-5608-ad21-162c3e1dd45a" :question/math-academy-id "q-9331" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[6]{{n}^{5}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{5/6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{5}{6}.$ Since $p\\le 1,$ the series is divergent.\n\nNote that the factor of $4$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence of the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "855a9c5c-9d01-584b-9b24-99834365b732" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a p-Series With Fractional Exponent Converges"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5a9a0ac-ec3f-5328-b510-e2373b85173e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a9a0ac-ec3f-5328-b510-e2373b85173e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262009} :answer-field/id #uuid "df143856-fb55-5527-a503-a0ef018edd46" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262008}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b19142ea-49a5-5749-b0b5-ee3907a47a2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-9337" :question/problem "Determine whether the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{n}^{4}}}$\nconverges or diverges.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The series can be written as\n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4/3}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a $p$-series with $p=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Since $p>1$, the series is convergent. \n\nNote that the factor of $2$ multiplying the series has no effect on the convergence/divergence \nof the series."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31ec9ae4-b0d6-5be9-bc8c-de29f3265f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262035} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ea8d3-f89e-5586-84d6-4f2fb5198b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262036} {:answer/id #uuid "a953b4c7-4ee6-5bc0-a8ac-ed73d3147732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262037} {:answer/id #uuid "2f42e654-6c96-54e8-b757-9b1eb76c5697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262038} {:answer/id #uuid "54293858-1d79-5dce-bab3-925acb13dc67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262039} {:answer/id #uuid "34a68151-c242-525f-a5da-8df577aa7e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262040} {:answer/id #uuid "9dffe7bf-50cd-5aa0-8f09-7c1dc592865a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262041} {:answer/id #uuid "112dd4e2-d13b-5fc2-92ec-691c04cd9869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54293858-1d79-5dce-bab3-925acb13dc67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262039} :answer-field/id #uuid "80ce5838-d2f1-5bb7-a228-f73d437d0ff4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262034}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84c8c29b-26d7-54c7-9b98-3b25f5c1151a" :question/math-academy-id "q-96680" :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $3{y}^{'}+2x{y}^{''}={x}^{2}y$\nII. - $5{x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{'}={e}^{x}$\nIII. - $4{({y}^{'})}^{3}+7{x}^{3}=x({y}^{2}+{({y}^{'})}^{2})$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260ab397-b927-5671-a132-7dd09d609d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262055} {:answer/id #uuid "7f06ae2a-7201-5a3b-99b4-8509ecf16acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262056} {:answer/id #uuid "8077e54d-7a37-5429-8fa9-ae9e74524229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262057} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd93856-caf5-588c-b235-bc48388d2a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262058} {:answer/id #uuid "22e76f62-d0c3-5a80-bbc8-c2dc099d27b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262059} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa4a613-9929-59a9-ba2b-6961e9f0f413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262060} {:answer/id #uuid "51c281f4-b71a-50f5-b47b-39e1bf734f00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262061} {:answer/id #uuid "65c7d200-aec2-5a6c-8d68-07f6d3c47302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f06ae2a-7201-5a3b-99b4-8509ecf16acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262056} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ce830f-f7ad-5a8f-80c9-bfdeca211ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262054}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22de21fe-5e7f-50a8-9618-79ceefd5ab17" :question/math-academy-id "q-96703" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=2$\nII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{ln}y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{2+3{y}^{3}}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-7{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=7{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b74d4121-19bd-519b-9f42-6e2d74a4e53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262065} {:answer/id #uuid "974d493e-45f3-5112-9946-af67f80df4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262066} {:answer/id #uuid "aae8832f-c0df-5015-9905-eda5a0e98849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262067} {:answer/id #uuid "8784430a-fefb-5a0e-8037-7072e097a072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262068} {:answer/id #uuid "e76fcc33-5ba1-50a2-b5ef-7bdb0d465af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262069} {:answer/id #uuid "59f18cbc-4413-5cee-a2a2-0a7277b9cd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262070} {:answer/id #uuid "710af5b4-256e-512a-a5d1-86c2b6361f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262071} {:answer/id #uuid "b70c79e3-f5b6-5da6-92fe-75d7cf1ec1e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8784430a-fefb-5a0e-8037-7072e097a072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b143ce9c-1d2f-54f8-9822-2eb13785f2e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262064}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f8c44a4-8695-5bce-9b85-93159ef8f779" :question/math-academy-id "q-96705" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. - ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\nII. - $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}y$\nIII. - $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "debfa9e3-f0d6-59a8-bd1a-a93ddedec8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262085} {:answer/id #uuid "d5192a67-97b2-58a1-918c-4a7ae001f7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262086} {:answer/id #uuid "59b7ad7c-c2a0-5250-8870-d1c274c642d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262087} {:answer/id #uuid "755b967b-454d-5af1-a6d7-4423160433e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262088} {:answer/id #uuid "7750975c-75a1-5e0e-b7af-140759e7ca53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262089} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4e9f1f-b4a5-52d2-a38c-f17ed129eff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262090} {:answer/id #uuid "e36f6f5b-7422-5092-a409-609d64e0adf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262091} {:answer/id #uuid "425d8c3b-9b21-5902-964b-634b329711ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7750975c-75a1-5e0e-b7af-140759e7ca53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262089} :answer-field/id #uuid "939e9ad9-80a2-515c-913c-6d8916123f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ed0d022-e8a6-5277-8016-371d657756f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-96987" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. - ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3$\nII. - $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,\\,{y}^{''}(0)=2$\nIII. - $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y},\\,y(0)=1$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{''}-y=2x$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(1)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $x{y}^{‴}-{e}^{x}{y}^{'}=9y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{'}(0)=1,$ and ${y}^{''}(0)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{'}-\\frac{1}{x}={e}^{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44d9ddf-a830-559a-807e-a5a3eaaf66e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6382687d-7aa3-5933-9ee6-b7bb4a90620f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6382687d-7aa3-5933-9ee6-b7bb4a90620f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262120} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66636a2-9208-5170-b91e-f795c24d0ed5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6efc25c1-7280-5213-84a4-9dd424baaae7" :question/math-academy-id "q-98152" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/4b445c27e9b4b720cfcc8b8b265485ba4f62106be39acc066cf041bf9db073b2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k{log}_{3}(bx).$ Find the value of $b+k.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{3}(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{3}1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(\\frac{1}{3},0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{3},0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,b=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{3}(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{3}(3\\cdot 1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k{log}_{3}3 & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb+k=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44d9ddf-a830-559a-807e-a5a3eaaf66e8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Transformed Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "918bdc14-d8d0-512b-9b65-820ae6d0ad5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "918bdc14-d8d0-512b-9b65-820ae6d0ad5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262123} :answer-field/id #uuid "289b11ae-f133-544e-a3cc-747a26d8b1cc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262122}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f292f735-dfaf-5103-802f-50b77b9a6c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-98153" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/60d178be10126417bfbfad4e7a316d43ad1fd94ca19ea72adae929500a7f13c2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the graph of $y=k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c.$ Find the value of $k+c.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k\\operatorname{ln}(1)+c \\\\  & =0+c \\\\  & =c.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(1,c)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(1,-2)$ lies on the curve. Hence,\n\n$$\n(1,-2)=(1,c)\\,\\Rightarrow \\,c=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(e,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\operatorname{ln}(x)+c & =y \\\\ k\\operatorname{ln}(e)-2 & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =2 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk+c=4-2=2.\n$$"}]}
